// PRReference is a single PR the session touched — created, opened, or
// discussed. A session can reference multiple PRs (e.g., split work, rebase
// onto a new branch+PR, or a Claude session used for two tasks).
// DIRECT_PR_REF attribution iterates every reference in
// UsageMetadata.pr_references, falling back to the scalar pr_url / pr_number
// / pr_repository fields on SessionMeta / UsageMetadata for older uploads.
// SignalSource says how a piece of session evidence was obtained, so the
// matcher can rank it rather than treating every value in a field alike.
//
// The distinction it encodes is whether the SESSION produced the value or the
// workspace merely happened to hold it. A `gh pr create` receipt is the
// session's own action; a PR URL scraped from a teammate's merge commit names a
// PR precisely and says nothing about this session. Both arrive in
// pr_references today and score identically, which is how one engineer's
// sessions came to display three colleagues' PRs.
//
// Rank, strongest first: SESSION_COMMAND > VENDOR_FIELD > SESSION_PROSE >
// LOCAL_GIT_WINDOWED > UNSPECIFIED — declaration order, with the zero value
// last. That alignment is a convenience, not a rule the code may lean on:
// proto additivity forces a new value to be APPENDED, so a source stronger than
// a local-git read would land at the weak end. Give any new value an explicit
// rank when you add it.
